# Copy to .release-request (gitignored) and edit, then: open tools/release.command
#
# FORMAT. This file is `.`-sourced as bash by tools/release.command, after
# release.env has already set PATH — so it is code, not config, and anything you
# write here executes with the toolchain on PATH. One NAME="value" per line;
# `#` comments and blank lines are fine. LF endings only: a CRLF copy leaves a
# trailing \r inside the last value, and the cut aborts on a component name it
# cannot match. Only COMPONENTS, FLAGS, CHANNEL and KEEP_WINDOW are read — a mistyped
# FLAG= is not an error, it is silently ignored, and the run proceeds as if you
# had set nothing.
#
# COMPONENTS — one or more of: cli gateway edge agent relay
#
# Cut in order. Each one's marker commit is pushed before the next starts —
# release.sh never pushes, and its release-origin guard refuses to cut while this
# repo is ahead of its remote, so an unpushed marker strands the rest.
#
# `all` is a real release.sh argument and is REFUSED here: it cuts every
# component inside one process with no push between them, so the batch ends
# sitting on several unpushed markers under a HEAD that reads [RELEASED: <last>]
# — the exact wedge the launcher exists to prevent. List them instead. Unknown
# names are refused the same way, before anything is built.
#
# FLAGS — required for a public cut. Accepted:
#
#     --public        Developer-ID signing + notarization AND the CVE gate
#     --apple         Developer-ID signing + notarization only
#     --vulncheck     the CVE gate only
#     --dry-run       build, sign and checksum into dist/ and stop: no tag, no
#                     GitHub Release, no upload, no marker commit — and the
#                     launcher skips its push path entirely
#     --bump-minor    move the minor instead of the default patch
#     --bump-major    move the major
#     --keep-version  pin versions/<comp> and mint a fresh stamp anyway; see the
#                     RUNBOOK section of that name. Refused in combination with
#                     --bump-minor, --bump-major or --force
#     --force         bump and cut even when the component source has not changed
#
# Leaving FLAGS empty is not "defaults", it is a trap. With neither --apple nor
# --vulncheck set, release.sh asks whether to cut a public release — and stdin IS
# a terminal in an opened .command, so an unattended run blocks on a prompt
# nobody is watching until someone closes the window. Answer it with anything but
# y, or let an empty answer through, and the cut publishes an AD-HOC signed,
# un-CVE-gated build to the PUBLIC channel, which is the worse of the two
# outcomes. Say what you want.
#
# A component whose source has not changed since its last cut resolves to the
# same stamp, whose tag already exists, and is refused. That is a no-op, not a
# failure to work around: drop it from COMPONENTS. Before reaching for --force,
# read the RUNBOOK's "Two releases can carry the same source SHA" — a re-cut from
# unchanged source creates a version pair that the stamp's SHA segment cannot
# tell apart, and triage pays for it later. If nothing in the component changed,
# what you actually want is a distribution-only republish, and that is NOT a
# launcher path:
#
#     bash tools/release.sh --distribute-only <comp> <stamp>
#
# run by hand. --distribute-only is recognised only as release.sh's FIRST
# argument, because it takes its component and stamp positionally — so it cannot
# be expressed in FLAGS at all.
#
# CHANNEL — stable (the default; also what an unset/absent CHANNEL= line
# means) or beta.
#
# Beta cuts from the registry's beta worktree instead of the main folder,
# stamps with a .beta. segment, skips GitHub entirely and uploads straight to
# R2 as a private, staged row — see the RUNBOOK's "Beta channel" section for
# opening/closing a cycle. --public is STILL required for a beta cut: a beta
# becomes publicly installable the moment the console promotes it, so signing,
# notarization and the CVE gate apply exactly as they do to stable. A beta
# cycle has to already be open (versions/<comp>.beta committed, the beta
# worktree present) — release.sh refuses with "open a beta cycle first"
# otherwise. Not compatible with --distribute-only (refused): that path
# republishes an already-GitHub-released component, which a beta cut never
# produces.
#
#     CHANNEL="beta"
#
# KEEP_WINDOW — hold the Terminal window open after the cut finishes.
#
# Unset (the default), what happens to this window is Terminal's decision, not
# the launcher's: the launcher exits, and the profile's "When the shell exits"
# setting acts on that.
#
#     Terminal ▸ Settings ▸ Profiles ▸ <your profile> ▸ Shell
#     "When the shell exits:"  Close the window / Close if the shell exited
#                              cleanly / Don't close the window
#
# The launcher READS that setting at the start of every run — it never writes it,
# and it never fails a cut over it — and puts one line in the log saying what it
# will mean, naming the profile it read. Two of the three settings were measured
# directly; the numbers stored in the plist are menu tags, not menu positions,
# and reading them the obvious way gets them backwards:
#
#   - "Close the window" closes it HOWEVER the cut ended. A cut that fails then
#     disappears off the screen, which is the one outcome this window exists to
#     prevent — so when this is the setting AND the cut fails, the launcher holds
#     the window itself, unasked, and says so. You do not have to remember to set
#     KEEP_WINDOW before the run that goes wrong.
#   - "Don't close the window" leaves it up after every cut. Nothing to hold;
#     close it yourself.
#   - "Close if the shell exited cleanly" is the setting you probably want, and
#     it is the one that was NOT measured — no profile on the machine this was
#     written on used it. The launcher reports an unrecognised value as
#     unrecognised instead of filing it under whichever branch looks likely.
#
# There is deliberately no scripted close. Asking Terminal to close the window
# over an Apple Event was built, tested and thrown away: sent from inside the
# running .command it left the window PINNED open — Terminal will not close a
# window whose tab still holds a process, and that process is the launcher — and
# a window asked once then stopped closing by any route at all, including the
# shell-exit setting that had been working before. Measured against a control
# that did nothing but exit 0 and closed every time. It also wanted an Automation
# grant nobody should have to give a release script. Hence: no Apple Events,
# nothing to allow, nothing to deny.
#
# Set KEEP_WINDOW=1 when you are debugging the cut itself and want the scrollback
# in front of you rather than in a file — release.sh's own output, the notarize
# chatter, and anything you were about to run in that window by hand. The only
# way a script can hold a window Terminal is about to close is to not finish, so
# the run stops at the end and waits for Return. That happens AFTER .release.log
# is written and closed, sentinel included, so an agent watching the log for
# RELEASE-EXIT: reads it and carries on while the window is still sitting there.
#
#     KEEP_WINDOW=1
#
# The launcher reads it from the environment too, but an opened .command does not
# inherit your shell's environment — LaunchServices does not carry it across — so
# this file is the setting that actually reaches a double-clicked or `open`ed run.

COMPONENTS="edge"
FLAGS="--public"
